\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For $C^*$-algebras $A,B\subseteq B(H)$, what structural properties and invariants are forced by small completely bounded Kadison–Kastler distance $d_{\mathrm{cb}}(A,B)$, and must sufficiently close algebras be isomorphic?
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2013/43}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
For $C^*$-algebras $A,B\subseteq B(H)$, what structural properties and invariants are forced by small completely bounded Kadison–Kastler distance $d_{\mathrm{cb}}(A,B)$, and must sufficiently close algebras be isomorphic?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
